Check the newline written by puts() for a broken pipe.
winansi_puts() wrote the string with winansi_fputs(), which exits with
status 141 when the output is a pipe whose reader has gone, but wrote
the newline with the C runtime's own putchar(), whose failure was not
checked. When writing the newline was what flushed the buffer and
failed, the broken pipe only set the stream's error flag, and whoami,
comm, grep and other applets that print with puts() carried on as if
the write had succeeded. Write the newline with winansi_putchar().

---
 win32/winansi.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/winansi.c
+++ b/win32/winansi.c
@@ -852,7 +852,8 @@
 
 int FAST_FUNC winansi_puts(const char *s)
 {
-	return (winansi_fputs(s, stdout) == EOF || putchar('\n') == EOF) ? EOF : 0;
+	return (winansi_fputs(s, stdout) == EOF ||
+				winansi_putchar('\n') == EOF) ? EOF : 0;
 }
 
 static sighandler_t sigpipe_handler = SIG_DFL;
